# Tamil People, Do Not Hesitate
Tamil title: தமிழினமே தயங்காதே

Closing address delivered by Chief Minister C. N. Annadurai at the Second World Tamil Conference in Madras on 10 January 1968.

Comrades,

At a moment when every heart is filled with Tamil, when breath and life are mingled with Tamil, when whatever we discuss becomes a discussion of Tamil and everything is compared with Tamil, what I deliver now will be an address only in name. I do not imagine it can truly be a grand address.

For ten days at Poompuhar we witnessed scenes, heard ideas and felt an uprising within our hearts. Will we see the same enthusiasm and inward awakening here tomorrow? We cannot.

## The Continuing Sound of Tamil

The Poompuhar conference made everyone ask: can it not somehow continue? Can the Tamil sound of these ten days not become permanent? Could conferences like this occur in every town? Could Tamil awakening arise in every Tamil mind?

At the outset, we did not expect Poompuhar to attain such distinction. We knew a language conference could be held, but did not fully expect lakhs of people to come and sit attentively for hours. The people have shown the depth of their love for Tamil.

Foreign visitors will return to Russia, Australia, America, Britain, Sri Lanka, Malaysia, Indonesia, Mauritius and Fiji and say: "We went to Tamil Nadu to research Tamil, but its people had studied it long before us, delighted in that study, loved the language above life itself, and regarded it as greater than every instrument of pleasure. They rejoice in its dignity and are prepared to give their precious lives if harm comes, or even appears likely to come, from any direction." They will say so wherever they have opportunity and right to speak.

Whatever part anyone played in this Poompuhar conference, these ten days will remain a lifelong source of pride.

You saw great poets and their poetic hearts. You heard their songs and speeches on Tamil and Tamil Nadu. You heard gifted speakers explain texts and connect their meaning with the thought and conditions of the present age.

## Chera, Chola and Pandya

One day a Chera may have stood upon this stage; another day a Chola walked here; on another a Pandya appeared. Ilango Adigal may have come, as did scholars of grammar.

The sacred name Poompuhar itself carried our thoughts far away. How splendidly we lived then, and in how many troubles we struggle now. Then we lived in abundance; today we have fallen into a pit of poverty. Then the world praised our life; today others come to seize what is ours, while a Chettinad ruler must tell us not to fear.

When the milk-white moon shines and its light falls upon us, poetic feeling rises even if we know neither metre nor grammar. We wish to sing of Tamil, which illuminates life. But when we enter the house and its only lamp goes out for lack of oil, surrounding us with darkness, what happens to the heart?

Should it praise or grieve? Moonlight outside, darkness within; an overflow of feeling outside, a broken heart within. It wavers and suffers.

For ten days, explanations by Tamil luminaries gave us pride like moonlight. When we think of the present, the heart becomes a house with its lamp extinguished.

Should I congratulate you for possessing sublime Tamil, or grieve that, despite such a language, prosperity has not entered our lives? Should I rejoice in a language that created the five great epics and other masterpieces, or mourn the condition in which some say another language is superior?

Tamil society functions upon the foundation of Tamil. We may feel that truth without knowing how to articulate it. But inwardly we know that Tamil people can live only if Tamil lives, and Tamil society only if Tamil people live.

That is why not only teachers, scholars and learned poets came to a language conference. All Tamil people came. They themselves appear as poetry. There is song in their speech, Tamil awakening in their breath, and when they move one feels heroic Tamil Nadu itself in motion.

Men and women, young and old, and mothers carrying victorious children all came. Mothers listened to the delight of Tamil while hoping that, whether or not their children understood the ideas, Tamil music would enter through their ears, mingle with their blood, and become one with their babbling speech.

A painter may try to depict a garden of buds and flowers, and a poet may dare turn it into verse. I am no painter; I tried to learn and failed. I have wished to compose poetry but lacked time. Yet what enters my heart remains in memory. I cannot forget the scene here either with eyes open or closed.

Poompuhar, the ancient-looking fort built in this city, its shining lamps, and the greater light streaming from your eyes and spreading beyond them will never leave my mind. You share that feeling.

Many conferences may follow, but you will ask whether any can equal Poompuhar. If told that a Tamil poet is coming to your town, you will ask whether it is one you did not already see here. You have formed an inseparable bond with Poompuhar and created Tamil awakening within yourselves.

At least because I ask tonight, begin writing poetry. Tamil feeling within you will certainly produce it.

## Everything Becomes Poetry

Scholars may say what you write is not poetry, judging by grammar. I judge by feeling and call it poetry. Your gaze is a poem, your speech a poem, your applause a poem, your greeting a poem, and the sound filling this place a poem.

What poet could sing it fully? A poet can sing of one flower blooming for a day, but who can sing of the flowers that have bloomed upon people's faces for ten days? A poet can sing of one white moon, but who can sing of these storyless lakhs of moons? Once you yourselves have become poetry, who can fashion you into a poem?

When the revolutionary actor M. G. R. said poetry contains beauty and inner feeling, you applauded. I considered what the applause meant and discovered that, because M. G. R. possesses beauty and feeling, you were applauding him as a poem.

For me, feeling is the measure of poetry. It may lack metre or grammar, though I do not say poetry should be without grammar. Feeling, beauty, purity, skilful action and the thought that gives rise to it, running above and through grammar, are themselves poetry.

Bharathidasan asked whether Tamil people who once ruled a State would govern again and offered the sweet news that Tamil government would come. Whether Tamils have truly acquired their own government requires extended debate. But you must agree that Tamils have begun to recognise themselves.

The Tamil character is neither to remain beneath anyone nor to degrade anyone. Tamil Nadu may be one part of the world, but we will never consent to its becoming anyone's hunting ground.

Ancient kings crossed the surging sea. Researchers now tell us that Tamil merchants sailed to Mauritius, Fiji and Madagascar and spread Tamil fragrance, though our ancient stories told us long ago.

Once we lived to the admiration of the world. Today we wait anxiously to learn who will give us what. Then we shone like the sun; today we struggle in a dark cave.

## Hesitation Must End

That does not mean the sun has vanished. I will not even say our eyes are damaged. Wipe dim eyes and look: the sun appears. We too can raise our heads and sing with Ilango Adigal, "Let us praise the sun, let us praise the sun."

I will not say Tamil Nadu has forgotten its natural dignity and fallen asleep or unconscious. I say only that it hesitates. We ask, "Can we do it?"

The Chera did not hesitate with that question when marching north. The Chola did not ask it when launching ships and conquering lands beyond the sea.

When a tiger leapt upon a Tamil village woman gathering grain in the forest, she did not ask whether she could prevail. She struck and drove it away with the winnowing tray in her hand. The tiger may have thought, "I am king of the forest and she merely queen of a house." Yet seeing her attack without spear or sword, using the tray with which she cleared refuse, it must have fled in shame, recognising Tamil Nadu and a Tamil woman's power.

Tamil women once drove away a tiger with a winnowing tray. They remain ready to drive away our enemies today, though today's enemy may first ask where that tray can be sold for cash.

Poompuhar is above all a conference that removes our hesitation. It teaches: "Do not hesitate, Tamil people." We must add another lesson: "Do not remain intoxicated, Tamil people."

For ten days we have heard Tamil's greatness and remained in its intoxication. It is an intoxication that does no harm, a delight in language perhaps uniquely available to Tamil people. Yet even from that harmless rapture we must emerge.

## Much Work Remains

There is much work, for everyone and every day. Forests must be reclaimed and prosperity increased. The unemployed must receive work and become skilled in productive occupations. We must dig the earth for minerals, and not only material objects but historical evidence.

Silted harbours must be restored. Tamil sailors should launch ships from them while singing Tamil poetry. Birds flying elsewhere should be drawn by the sweet music and turn toward those ships, spreading their wings above them like a canopy.

Tamil sailors should sing the birds' songs, and birds the sailors' music. The birds should say: "We possess pure hearts; therefore we too belong to the Tamil kind. We rise higher and higher; that is Tamil dignity."

We imagine that day. Valluvar says what we intend will happen if our resolve is firm. I cannot quote him with Navalar's majesty, but I give you the essence: what we conceive in this world will happen if we become steadfast.

Along with "Do not hesitate" and "Do not remain intoxicated," Tamil people must become resolute. With that resolve, everything they undertake can succeed, and a day will come when we announce ourselves to the world.

For such resolve, look to Kannagi, heroine of the *Silappathikaram*. Weeping for justice and holding the anklet, she entered the royal court and challenged the Pandya king Nedunchezhiyan: "O king whose reason has failed!" She possessed inward certainty.

She was a woman who had lost her husband, yet she did not merely weep, nor think of surviving quietly and beginning another life. Kannagi was the chaste deity of the fertile Chola land who fought for justice. Even after losing her husband, she vowed not to permit a stain upon his name, entered the court and thundered against the unreasoning king.

Why should we not acquire the courageous feeling given by that Tamil heroine? The unveiling of Kannagi's statue upon the beach teaches precisely this.

A woman who had lost her husband and possessed no companion but reason addressed a king as one bereft of understanding. We must recover her feeling and awakening.

This wealth of knowledge is what we can give the world, wealth that never diminishes through giving.

Carry to every town and every good person the beautiful scenes, processions and celebrations of these ten days, and the radiance they created within and upon your faces. Tell everyone; through hearing this good news, even those not yet good may become so.

Go as sacred messengers carrying that message. Return victorious and bring prosperity to Tamil Nadu.

---NOTES---
The OCR prints the date as “10-71-1968.” The event is the closing of the Second World Tamil Conference in Madras, supporting 10 January 1968; the source remains verbatim.

The printed account repeatedly calls the venue or festival space “Poompuhar.” This is retained as the source's own naming within the Madras conference setting.

The phrase “மயங்காதே தமிழா” carries both “do not lose awareness” and “do not remain intoxicated.” The latter is used because Anna explicitly describes the harmless intoxication of linguistic delight before insisting upon practical work.

The tiger-and-winnowing-tray story and historical maritime claims are translated as cultural and rhetorical references in the source, not independently authenticated factual claims.

The Kannagi address “அறிவு அறை போகிய அரசனே” is rendered as a king whose reason has failed, preserving its accusation of failed judgment rather than translating each word mechanically.
